---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { label: "Start a review", href: "#start" },
  { label: "Choosing a provider", href: "#provider" },
  { label: "Review findings", href: "#findings" },
  { label: "Approve or revise", href: "#approve" },
  { label: "Review history", href: "#history" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Code review"
  description="Run review through the coding provider you already use, inspect findings by file and severity, and keep the decision beside the diff."
  currentPath="/docs/code-review"
  eyebrow="Product"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="start">
    <h2>Start a review</h2>
    <p>
      Open a task with a working diff, choose Code Review, and select an
      available provider. The review runs natively through that provider and is
      persisted with the project.
    </p>
    <p>
      Confirm the review scope before starting: the current working diff,
      selected commits, or the change relative to a target branch. A narrow,
      explicit scope produces findings that are easier to verify.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="provider">
    <h2>Choosing a provider</h2>
    <p>
      Code review runs through one of your connected coding-agent providers,
      not a separate Modesto-hosted model — the same way chat threads do. A
      review from Claude Code, Codex, or another provider will read the diff
      differently and phrase findings differently; there's no single "the"
      review model. If you're unsure, run review with the same provider that
      wrote the change and a second, different provider for an independent
      pass — disagreement between the two is itself a useful signal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="findings">
    <h2>Review findings</h2>
    <p>
      Findings stream while the review runs and are grouped by file and
      severity. Open a finding beside the relevant diff, inspect the evidence,
      and decide whether it blocks the change.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>Blocking</strong><span>Correctness, security, or reliability issues that should be resolved before merge.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Important</strong><span>Material maintainability or behavior concerns requiring deliberate review.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Suggestion</strong><span>Non-blocking improvements worth considering.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="approve">
    <h2>Approve or revise</h2>
    <p>
      Request changes with a targeted follow-up, approve when the diff and
      checks are ready, or merge from the same task surface once repository
      policy allows it.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout"><strong>Review does not expand permissions.</strong><p>The review provider uses the run’s configured capabilities. Approval records a decision; merge still follows repository access and branch policy.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="history">
    <h2>Review history</h2>
    <p>
      Modesto keeps each review run and its findings with the task revision it
      inspected. When a follow-up changes the diff, rerun review where the
      change is material and resolve prior findings against the new evidence
      rather than silently discarding them.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
